<h2>The rule and its rationale</h2>
<p>Article 136 confers a discretionary power to grant special leave against any order of any court or tribunal. From the beginning, the Supreme Court has administered that power with self-imposed limits, of which the concurrent-findings rule is the most consequential in everyday practice. Its logic is institutional: facts are found in the forums designed for fact-finding, with oral evidence, cross-examination and full records; the Supreme Court&rsquo;s function is to correct errors of law, resolve conflicts of principle, and intervene where injustice is manifest. Concurrence between two courts is treated as strong assurance that the factual foundation is sound — a presumption, not an immunity.</p>

<h2>Where the door opens</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Perversity.</strong> A finding so unreasonable on the evidence that no properly instructed court could have reached it. This is the broadest exception and the most abused label; the Court expects demonstration, not characterisation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No evidence.</strong> A conclusion resting on nothing — a fact assumed, an inference without foundation. Distinct from insufficient evidence, which remains within the lower courts&rsquo; domain.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Ignored or inadmissible material.</strong> Vital evidence overlooked — an unimpeached document, an admission — or reliance on material the law excludes. The ignored item must be shown to be decisive, not merely helpful.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Misconstruction at the root.</strong> Construction of a document is often a question of law; where the courts below misread the core instrument — a will, a deed, a contract — concurrence does not protect the error.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Wrong legal standard.</strong> Burden of proof misplaced, a statutory presumption ignored or misapplied, the wrong test for cruelty, adverse possession or readiness and willingness: findings produced by bad law are not findings of fact at all.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting for the first hearing</h2>
<p>A concurrent-findings SLP is usually decided in minutes at the admission stage, so the petition must put the infirmity on the surface. Three disciplines help. First, isolate: challenge the one or two findings that actually decide the case, not every adverse observation. Second, anchor: every assertion of perversity should carry a pinpoint reference to the deposition or exhibit, with the relevant passage extracted in the petition or its annexures — the Bench will not excavate the record. Third, connect: show that correcting the finding changes the outcome. A petition that establishes a flaw in a peripheral finding earns a dismissal with the observation that the result stands regardless.</p>

<h2>The respondent&rsquo;s playbook</h2>
<p>For respondents defending concurrent findings, the strategy is the mirror image: open with the concurrence itself; demonstrate that the so-called perversity is a request for reappreciation; and show that each piece of &ldquo;ignored&rdquo; evidence was either considered or immaterial. A caveat filed in anticipation ensures the respondent is heard before any interim order — particularly important where the decree under challenge is executable, since an ex parte stay can freeze the fruits of a decade of litigation.</p>
<div class="note">The concurrent-findings rule operates at the leave stage; where leave is granted, the appeal is heard like any civil or criminal appeal within the scope the Court defines. Nothing in this article is a prediction about any matter — whether a particular record discloses perversity is itself the whole contest, and it turns on the specific evidence.</div>
